# AGENTS.md

Operating instructions for any coding agent working in {{ repo }}. This file is the
single source of truth; every other agent-instruction file in this repo points here
rather than restating it.

Generated by `nice-and-tidy`. Edit it freely — a later run will show you a diff and
ask before touching your changes, never overwrite them silently. Project-specific
values come from `nice-and-tidy.config.json`.

Sections marked **if you can** describe something not every agent is able to do. Do
the part you can do, skip the part you can't, and say which.

---

## 1. Work starts from an issue

Before writing code for a new feature, bug, or change: an issue exists for it first.
From that point on, the issue is where the work is tracked — priority, labels,
milestone, status.

**If you can run shell commands**, create it yourself and fill every field that
applies: title, body, labels, milestone, assignee (default: {{ assignee }}), and the
board fields. **If you can't**, write the issue title and body into your reply and
say it needs creating before work begins.

Never invent an issue number. If no issue exists and you cannot create one, describe
the work instead — a made-up number is worse than an honest gap.

One issue per session. A session that touches three unrelated issues produces a diff
nobody can review and a handoff nobody can read.

## 2. Branches

```
<type>/<issue-number>-<kebab-case-slug>
```

Examples: `feature/12-search-index`, `fix/31-stale-avatar-cache`,
`docs/8-contributing-guide`, `chore/19-bump-node`.

- Cut everyday branches from **`{{ baseBranch }}`** and open the pull request back
  into **`{{ baseBranch }}`**. This repo uses a {{ branchModel }} model.
- Pick the type from what the branch's commits will mostly be, not from the issue's
  label. An issue labelled `bug` whose fix turns out to be documentation-only ships
  on a `docs/` branch.
- The issue number in the name is what makes `git branch --list '*31*'` work and lets
  the branch answer "what is this for" without opening a browser.
- Never push directly to `{{ productionBranch }}`.

## 3. Commits

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>

<footer>
```

- **type** — required, from the table below.
- **scope** — optional. For this repo: {{ commitScopes }}. Omit it when the change is
  repo-wide.
- **subject** — imperative mood, lowercase, no trailing period. "add the gate," not
  "added" or "adds". Long enough to carry the *why* when brevity would hide it.
- **body** — optional, wrapped near 72 columns. Says why; the diff already says what.
- **footer** — `Refs #N` to reference an issue, `Closes #N` to close one (see §4),
  `BREAKING CHANGE: …` where it applies.

| Type | Use for |
|---|---|
| `feat` | A new user-facing capability. |
| `fix` | A bug fix. |
| `docs` | Documentation only — no code touched. |
| `style` | Formatting, whitespace, punctuation — no logic change. |
| `refactor` | A code change that is neither a fix nor a feature. |
| `perf` | A change made specifically to improve performance. |
| `test` | Adding or correcting tests, with no production code change. |
| `build` | The build system or its config. |
| `ci` | CI/CD pipeline changes. |
| `chore` | Repo housekeeping that fits no type above. |
| `dependencies` | A dependency added, removed, or bumped — kept separate from `chore` so `git log --grep '^dependencies'` finds every one. |
| `revert` | Reverts a previous commit. Subject: `revert: <original subject>`; body names the reverted hash. |

No `wip`, no `misc`. A commit that is hard to type is usually two commits.

**Nothing enforces this.** There is no commit-message linter in this repo. It is a
convention, and this file will not claim otherwise. If drift becomes a real problem,
a commit-message hook is the natural fix.

## 4. Pull requests

Fill every field that applies: linked issue, labels, assignee (default:
{{ assignee }}), milestone, and the board fields. Fill every section of the pull
request template — a section left as its guidance comment is not filled.

Linking so the issue closes on merge:

- `Closes #N` / `Fixes #N` / `Resolves #N` in the **PR body** closes the issue on
  merge. A bare `#N` only references it.
- GitHub honours those keywords **only when the PR merges into the repository's
  default branch.** If everyday PRs land on a branch that is not the default, the
  keyword silently closes nothing — check which branch is the default before relying
  on it, and close the issue by hand otherwise.

## 5. Board status

Keep the issue's status matching reality, at the moment reality changes:

| Reality | Status |
|---|---|
| Not started | Backlog / Ready |
| Branch exists, no PR open | In progress |
| PR open | In review |
| PR merged | Done |

Closing a PR does not move the linked issue's status on its own — GitHub closes the
issue but leaves the board field alone. Move it.

**If you can run shell commands**, re-query field and option IDs before using them.
Never reuse an ID from memory or from an older document; a stale ID writes the wrong
value to the wrong field without erroring.

## 6. How to reply

Concise, complete, straight to the point. State results and decisions directly. Don't
restate the request, don't narrate the steps you're about to take, don't pad.

Default verbosity costs the reader attention they didn't agree to spend. That is the
reason this is on by default, and it is not an apology.

End every substantive reply with a compact status block. **Omit any line that has
nothing to report — never print "None" as filler.**

- **Questions pending** — what you need answered, as brief bullets.
- **Progress** — % complete on the current task or milestone.
- **Git/PR status** — branch, clean or uncommitted, PR number and state.
- **Expected next input** — what the user should say next. **If your session has a
  way to compact its own context or hand off to a fresh session**, and this
  conversation has grown long, recommend doing that here.

## 7. Ending a session

At a real stopping point — not after every message — add:

- **Done this session** — brief, complete bullets.
- **Planned for next session** — brief, complete bullets.
- **Memory handoff** — enough state that the next session can pick up cold.

Before signing off: push everything, update the issue and PR, leave the working tree
clean. This is a handoff, not a pause.

### Where memory goes

Session memory is written to **`{{ memoryFile }}`** and nowhere else.

Not to issue comments. Not to PR comments. Not to board notes. GitHub stays exactly
as readable to a human as it would be if no agent had ever touched this repo.

The distinction is between project data and commentary:

- **Issue and PR *fields*** — status, labels, milestone, assignee, links — are real
  project data. Keep updating them on GitHub, exactly as §1–§5 describe.
- **An agent's running account of its own sessions** — what it tried, what it
  learned, where it stopped — is not project data. It goes in the memory file.

This is not a preference that a sufficiently capable agent gets to override. There is
no GitHub-comment option.

## 8. Non-negotiables

- Say what isn't enforced. If a rule here is convention rather than a gate, the
  generated docs say so, and so should you.
- Never fabricate a GitHub field ID, option ID, or issue number. Re-query.
- Never flip the repository's default branch, delete a label, or close a tracking
  issue unless it was asked for in the conversation. Prepare the command and print
  it; a human runs it.
- Never overwrite a hand-edited file without showing what would change and asking.
